From: OpenWrt packaging fix
Subject: CMakeLists.txt: fix NEON detection/flags for cross-compilation

Same class of bug found (and fixed) in the old ha7ilm/csdr Makefile
and in the fftw3 OpenWrt package: NEON is detected by reading
/proc/cpuinfo via execute_process() at CMake configure time, which
runs on the *build host*, not the cross-compilation target - always
wrong for a real OpenWrt cross build (an x86_64 build server has no
"neon" in its /proc/cpuinfo regardless of what the target CPU has).

Also, on the ARM/x86_64/aarch64 branches this file *overwrote*
CMAKE_C_FLAGS/CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS with SET(...) instead of appending to
them - discarding whatever the caller (OpenWrt's cmake.mk, which
passes TARGET_CFLAGS - the target's -Os, hardening flags, etc.) had
already put there.

Fix (part 1): introduce an explicit CSDR_HAS_NEON cache option that
a cross-compiling packager sets directly (OpenWrt's package Makefile
sets it from CONFIG_CPU_TYPE, the reliable Kconfig-level signal -
see csdr2/Makefile), keep the /proc/cpuinfo probe only as a
best-effort native-build fallback when the option isn't set
explicitly, and append rather than overwrite CMAKE_C_FLAGS/
CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS throughout.

Fix (part 2): drop the inherited "-Wformat=0" from the ARM/aarch64
flag sets. Appending TARGET_CFLAGS (part 1, above) means OpenWrt's
own "-Wformat -Werror=format-security" now also ends up on the
command line - and since this project's own "-Wformat=0" used to be
appended *after* it, the net effect was -Wformat getting turned back
off post-hoc, leaving -Werror=format-security with nothing to apply
to. GCC reports that specific contradiction as an error in its own
right, which aborts the build under -Werror. This is a real error,
caught building on real OpenWrt hardware:
  cc1plus: error: '-Wformat-security' ignored without '-Wformat' [-Werror=format-security]
"-Wformat=0" was only ever suppressing harmless printf format-type
warnings (mismatched integer widths etc, same as the old csdr
codebase has) - never required for correctness, and OpenWrt's own
-Wformat/-Werror=format-security already showed that same class of
warning as non-fatal on the original csdr codebase, so removing it
doesn't trade one problem for another.

Verified: reproduced the exact error with our own ARM cross
toolchain by replaying the logged flags verbatim, confirmed removing
"-Wformat=0" resolves it, then cross-compiled the *entire* source
tree for arm-linux-gnueabihf with -DCSDR_HAS_NEON=ON using OpenWrt's
real logged flag set (down to the literal "-Wformat
-Werror=format-security -fstack-protector -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=1"
hardening flags): all 42 translation units in libcsdr compile with
zero errors. Link fails only because this sandbox has no ARM-built
fftw3f/libsamplerate available, not a source-level error.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -53,27 +53,45 @@
     set(CSDR_IMA_ADPCM true)
 endif()
 
+if(NOT DEFINED CSDR_HAS_NEON)
+    # Default (native/non-cross builds): keep the original best-effort
+    # /proc/cpuinfo probe. This is NOT meaningful when cross-compiling -
+    # it reads the *build host's* cpuinfo, not the target's - so
+    # cross-compiling packagers (e.g. OpenWrt) should pass
+    # -DCSDR_HAS_NEON=ON/OFF explicitly instead of relying on this.
+    if (EXISTS "/proc/cpuinfo")
+        execute_process(COMMAND "cat" "/proc/cpuinfo" OUTPUT_VARIABLE CPUINFO)
+        string(FIND "${CPUINFO}" "neon" NEON_POSITION)
+        if(NEON_POSITION GREATER_EQUAL 0)
+            set(CSDR_HAS_NEON ON)
+        else()
+            set(CSDR_HAS_NEON OFF)
+        endif()
+    else()
+        set(CSDR_HAS_NEON OFF)
+    endif()
+endif()
+set(CSDR_HAS_NEON ${CSDR_HAS_NEON} CACHE BOOL "Target has a NEON FPU (must be set explicitly when cross-compiling)")
+
 if (CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR STREQUAL "x86_64")
-    SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "-ffast-math -mfpmath=sse")
-    SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "-ffast-math -mfpmath=sse")
+    SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -ffast-math -mfpmath=sse")
+    SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS} -ffast-math -mfpmath=sse")
 elseif(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES ^arm.*)
-    execute_process(COMMAND "cat" "/proc/cpuinfo" OUTPUT_VARIABLE CPUINFO)
-    string(FIND ${CPUINFO} "neon" NEON_POSITION)
-    if(NEON_POSITION GREATER_EQUAL 0)
+    if (CSDR_HAS_NEON)
         set(HAS_NEON true)
     endif()
     if (HAS_NEON)
-        SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "-ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations -Wformat=0 -mfloat-abi=hard -mfpu=neon -mvectorize-with-neon-quad")
-        SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "-ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations -Wformat=0 -mfloat-abi=hard -mfpu=neon -mvectorize-with-neon-quad")
+        SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations -mfloat-abi=hard -mfpu=neon -mvectorize-with-neon-quad")
+        SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS} -ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations -mfloat-abi=hard -mfpu=neon -mvectorize-with-neon-quad")
     else()
-        SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "-ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations -Wformat=0")
-        SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "-ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations -Wformat=0")
+        SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations")
+        SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS} -ffast-math -march=armv7-a -mtune=cortex-a8 -funsafe-math-optimizations")
     endif()
 elseif(CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR MATCHES ^aarch64.*)
     # aarch64 mandated neon
     set(HAS_NEON true)
-    SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "-ffast-math -march=armv8-a -mtune=cortex-a72 -funsafe-math-optimizations -Wformat=0")
-    SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "-ffast-math -march=armv8-a -mtune=cortex-a72 -funsafe-math-optimizations -Wformat=0")
+    SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -ffast-math -march=armv8-a -mtune=cortex-a72 -funsafe-math-optimizations")
+    SET(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS} -ffast-math -march=armv8-a -mtune=cortex-a72 -funsafe-math-optimizations")
 endif()
 
 SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE "-O3")
